**I found that making these functions thread-safe negatively impacts clock accuracy and decided to see why that is the case.**

This helps because the start time variable is used in math. Unfortunately, the time-since-start functions can't be profiled (even with something like Wallclock.h) because the game can't start if you try to add logging anywhere inside the time-since-start functions. The best I could do is measure tiny increments of time by adding logging to GameLoop. The best I can do as for proof of why this works is to demonstrate how frequently different threads are attempting to access this variable.

**Profiling shows that from game launch to the end of a single song, about 30 different threads attempt to access `g_iStartTime`. This value is consistent whether you start up the game and get right into the first song that's in the song wheel, or take your time choosing a song and going thru menus before playing.**

Each song played results in 3 or more new thread local variables for `g_iStartTime`.

To prevent bottlenecks caused by multiple threads attempting to access this variable, I've made it atomic and created a thread-local variable to carry `g_iStartTime`, so that each thread has its own copy of that variable.

After 30 minutes of gameplay and ~5 minutes spent in Edit Mode, the number of thread-local variables created reached 80.

Note the `thread_local` specifier indicates that the variable will be removed after the thread is terminated, so we don't need a way to manage those variables.

This was my profiling code added to `RageTimer.cpp`:
```
// Global atomic start time for initialization
static std::atomic<std::uint64_t> g_iGlobalStartTime = ArchHooks::GetMicrosecondsSinceStart(true);
static std::atomic<int> g_threadLocalCounter(0);

// Thread-local start time
thread_local std::uint64_t g_iThreadStartTime = []() {
    g_threadLocalCounter++;
    return g_iGlobalStartTime.load();
}();

// Function to get the current count of thread-local variables
int RageTimer::GetThreadLocalCount() {
    return g_threadLocalCounter.load();
}
```

And then in GameLoop.cpp I called that function in the end of `UpdateAllButDraw`:
```
LOG->Trace("GameLoop::UpdateAllButDraw() done");
LOG->Trace("RageTimer::GetLocalThreadCount() = %i", RageTimer::GetThreadLocalCount());
```

